WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.400
This is the pH scale we’d looked at in the
first part of the video:

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.500
The acidic nature increases as we move towards
the left, and the basic nature

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:20.220
increases as we move towards the right. Higher
the concentration of H plus ions,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:27.260
lower will be the pH. And lower concentration
of H plus ions will imply higher pH

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.320
value.
The pH scale basically tells us the strength

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:37.360
of an acid or a base! The ‘p’ in pH
stands for potenz which means ‘power’

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.940
or ‘potential’ in german. We can
understand it as the power of H plus ions.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.320
More the number of H plus ions, more
acidic it’ll be.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.270
Usually, a paper impregnated with the universal
indicator is used to measure the

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:59.449
pH. Before we understand what a universal
indicator is, we need to have a basic

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:04.710
idea of what indicators are!
Substances which show the acidic or basic

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:11.250
behavior of other substances, by
changing in some way, are known as indicators.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.970
These are some of the common types of indicators:
Natural, Olfactory, Synthetic, Universal

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:20.270
As the name suggests, indicators obtained
from natural sources are called natural

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:27.240
indicators. Litmus and turmeric are examples
of natural indicators. Litmus is

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:34.080
obtained from lichens. If you remember, an
acid turns blue litmus paper red and a

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.399
base turns red litmus paper blue!
Substances which change their SMELL when mixed

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:45.641
with acid or base are
referred to as olfactory indicators. Onion

00:01:45.641 --> 00:01:51.550
and vanilla are examples of
olfactory indicators. Paste or juice of onion

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:57.209
loses its smell when added with
base. It does not change its smell with acid.

00:01:57.209 --> 00:02:01.369
And the smell of vanilla vanishes
with base, but its smell does not vanish with

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:07.050
an acid. Do you know why
they’re used? They are used to ensure the

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:12.790
participation of visually impaired
students in the laboratory. Interesting isn’t

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:16.920
it?
What are Synthetic Indicators?&nbsp;Indicators

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.280
that are SYNTHESIZED in the
laboratory are known as synthetic indicators.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.890
Phenolphthalein and methyl
orange are two examples of synthetic indicators.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.740
Phenolphthalein is a
colorless liquid and remains colorless with

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:38.720
acid, but turns pink with a base.
Methyl orange is originally orange in color

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.000
and turns red with acid and yellow
with base.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.450
If you noticed, the following indicators only
tell us the acidic or basic

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:52.240
character of a solution! But it doesn’t
tell us the STRENGTH of the acid or

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.320
base! That’s what brings us to the universal
indicator!

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.250
A universal indicator shows different colors
over the range of pH values from

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:08.970
1 to 14 for a given solution. And it is available
in the form of strips as well as

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:12.290
solutions. This is how the color matching
chart looks!

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:18.400
pH values from zero to six show acidic nature.
And pH values from eight to

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:25.190
fourteen show basic behavior. And a pH of
seven shows that it’s purely

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.490
neutral!
Let’s see what colors some of the most common

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:34.240
substances result in!
Do you know the acid that forms in our stomach?

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:40.459
Yes gastric acid! It has a pH
of about one point two. And that tells us

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:47.260
how acidic it is!
Lemon juice has a pH of around 2.2 to 2.4.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:53.060
What about coffee? It has pH of
about 5. It’s weakly acidic!

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:59.600
Distilled water has a pH of 7. Totally neutral.
And human blood has a pH of

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:06.680
about 7.3 to 7.5.
Baking soda has a pH of around 8.3 which tells

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:13.360
us that its weakly alkali.
Household ammonia has a pH of around 12. Yes

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.750
very alkali! And a sodium
hydroxide solution has a pH of close to 14.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:23.440
Extremely basic!
In the next video, we will understand the

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:24.280
importance of pH in everyday life!

